# Postgres. These are the names for a FRESH CloudHouse setup — create the
# role and database first:
#   createuser -h localhost -P cloudhouse     # then: createdb -h localhost -O cloudhouse cloudhouse
#
# EXISTING INSTALLATION: the role and database are still named `ambikagold`
# (the rebrand did not rename live Postgres objects, so the data stays put).
# If you are pointing at that database, set all three back to ambikagold or
# just delete this file — internal/platform/config.go already defaults to the
# ambikagold role/database when no .env is present.
DB_HOST=localhost
DB_PORT=5432
DB_USER=cloudhouse_gold
DB_PASSWORD=cloudhouse_gold
DB_NAME=cloudhouse_gold
DB_SSLMODE=disable

# Per-connection guards on the shared database. Empty = no limit (Postgres's
# own default, and how this ran before). Every business's rows live in the same
# tables on the same server, so one runaway query or abandoned open
# transaction holds locks and connection slots every other business needs.
#
# Left unset here on purpose: migrate.Run builds indexes and rewrites tables
# on this same pool, and a timeout short enough to catch a bad report is
# short enough to abort an index build partway. Set them in production AND
# run `go run ./cmd/migrate` with them unset.
# DB_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT=30s
# DB_IDLE_TX_TIMEOUT=60s
PORT=8080
FRONTEND_DIR=../frontend/out
FRONTEND_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3000,http://localhost:3100
# Encrypts integration API keys stored via the Settings page (see
# internal/settings). Must be a base64-encoded 32-byte key, e.g. generate
# one with: openssl rand -base64 32
# If unset, a fixed dev-only key is used (fine for local dev, NOT for
# production — set a real value before storing real secrets).
SETTINGS_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# Encrypts each customer's Aadhaar number at rest (see internal/customer).
# Same format as SETTINGS_ENCRYPTION_KEY, but MUST be a separate key —
# generate with: openssl rand -base64 32
# If unset, a fixed dev-only key is used (fine for local dev, NOT for
# production — set a real value before storing real customer data).
AADHAAR_ENCRYPTION_KEY=
# Local-disk folder for uploaded files (customer photos/KYC documents,
# ornament photos), served back under /uploads. Defaults to ./uploads.
UPLOADS_DIR=
# Signs/verifies login JWTs (see internal/auth). Set a long random secret —
# generate one with: openssl rand -base64 32
# If unset, a fixed dev-only secret is used (fine for local dev, NOT for
# production).
JWT_SECRET=
# How long an issued login token stays valid. Accepts a normal Go duration
# (e.g. "168h") or "<n>d" (e.g. "3d", "7d"). Defaults to 168h (7 days) if
# unset/unparseable.
JWT_EXPIRY=
# SMTP settings for outgoing password-reset emails (see platform/mailer.go).
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
SMTP_FROM=
# Twilio credentials for outgoing SMS/WhatsApp customer notifications (see
# internal/notification). ACCOUNT_SID is always required (identifies the
# account). Authenticate with EITHER the account Auth Token OR a scoped API
# Key SID/Secret pair (recommended — revocable without rotating the whole
# account's Auth Token). FROM_NUMBER must be a Twilio-provisioned number
# (E.164 format, e.g. +15551234567).
TWILIO_ACCOUNT_SID=
TWILIO_AUTH_TOKEN=
TWILIO_API_KEY_SID=
TWILIO_API_KEY_SECRET=
TWILIO_FROM_NUMBER=
# Surepass KYC credentials (see internal/platform/surepass.go,
# https://app.surepass.app/docs/kyc). One bearer token covers PAN verification,
# and DigiLocker Aadhaar verification (eSign has its own token, below). Indemnity
# bond PDF rendering needs Chrome/Chromium on this host — see README's
# "Indemnity bond eSign" setup note.
# Use the sandbox token with the sandbox URL and the production token with the
# production URL: https://sandbox.surepass.app or https://kyc-api.surepass.app.
# Defaults to the sandbox host if unset.
SUREPASS_API_TOKEN=
SUREPASS_BASE_URL=https://sandbox.surepass.app
# Surepass Suresign eSign (https://docs.esign.surepass.app) — a separate product
# from the KYC APIs above, with its own host and its own bearer token. Digitally
# signs the loan indemnity bond (internal/loan/indemnitybond.go).
SUREPASS_ESIGN_BASE_URL=https://prod-esign-api-v2.surepass.app
SUREPASS_ESIGN_TOKEN=
# Fallback eSign stamp position (PDF points from the top-left of an A4 page),
# used only when the rendered bond has no measured signature lines.
SUREPASS_ESIGN_SIGN_X=103
SUREPASS_ESIGN_SIGN_Y=478
# Suresign measures a stamp's y from the BOTTOM of the page; the app converts the
# top-measured positions above/measured from the bond. Set to false only if
# stamps land mirrored top-to-bottom after a signing run.
SUREPASS_ESIGN_Y_FROM_BOTTOM=true
# Cashfree Secure ID credentials — sandbox test keys for now (Aadhaar OTP
# verification: offline-aadhaar/otp + offline-aadhaar/verify). Not yet
# wired into any code — added to .env only, per explicit instruction not to
# use/test until confirmed.
CASHFREE_CLIENT_ID=
CASHFREE_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Razorpay. RAZORPAY_KEY_ID/SECRET already back customer-app loan payments
# (internal/platform/razorpay.go's Orders API). RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET is
# a SEPARATE secret — set on the Razorpay dashboard's webhook settings when
# configuring the /api/v1/webhooks/razorpay endpoint for Subscriptions
# events (plan Step 10, internal/billing) — never the same value as
# RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET. Left empty, every webhook is refused rather than
# trusted unverified.
RAZORPAY_KEY_ID=
RAZORPAY_KEY_SECRET=
RAZORPAY_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# OpenWA (./OpenWA) location — see internal/webstatic's WhatsApp dashboard
# proxy. Defaults to the colocated dev instance.
WHATSAPP_ORIGIN=http://127.0.0.1:2785
# Temporary .env fallback for OpenWASender.Send, used only until Settings >
# WhatsApp (the api_key_settings "whatsapp" row) is configured — once that
# row exists it takes over and these are ignored. Get these from the OpenWA
# dashboard (/admin/whatsapp): API Keys for WHATSAPP_API_KEY, Sessions for
# WHATSAPP_SESSION_ID.
WHATSAPP_API_KEY=
WHATSAPP_SESSION_ID=

# Connection pool. platform.PinTenantConn holds ONE connection for the whole of
# each authenticated request (Row-Level Security reads a per-connection session
# variable, so the connection cannot be shared), which makes this the effective
# ceiling on concurrent authenticated requests. Raise it together with
# Postgres's own max_connections, never past it.
# DB_MAX_OPEN_CONNS=25
# DB_MAX_IDLE_CONNS=5

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Row-Level Security: the application must NOT be a superuser
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Tenant isolation is enforced twice: by the filter in Go
# (internal/platform/tenant_gorm.go) and by PostgreSQL policies applied in
# internal/migrate/rls.go. The second layer only bites for a NON-SUPERUSER role
# — superusers bypass every policy, and FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY does not
# change that. The server logs a loud warning at boot when this is not met.
#
# Create the application role once, then point DB_USER at it:
#
#   CREATE ROLE cloudhouse_app LOGIN PASSWORD '<secret>' NOSUPERUSER NOCREATEDB NOCREATEROLE;
#   GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA public TO cloudhouse_app;
#   GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA public TO cloudhouse_app;
#   GRANT USAGE, SELECT ON ALL SEQUENCES IN SCHEMA public TO cloudhouse_app;
#   ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA public
#     GRANT SELECT, INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE ON TABLES TO cloudhouse_app;
#   ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA public
#     GRANT USAGE, SELECT ON SEQUENCES TO cloudhouse_app;
#
# Keep running `go run ./cmd/migrate` as the OWNER (the role that created the
# tables): migrations must see and alter every business's rows.

# --- Superadmin console bootstrap -------------------------------------------
# Opens the very first door to the superadmin console on a FRESH database, so
# there is something to sign in to before any business exists.
#
# Deliberately NOT defaulted in code: this account can create, suspend and
# re-tier every business on the deployment, and there is no safe password to
# bake into a repository for that. Leave these unset and the console simply
# has no way in until a superadmin user is seeded by hand — the tenant-facing
# product still boots and serves normally.
#
# Create-only. Once any superadmin user exists these are ignored entirely,
# even if they differ from what is stored, because from that point the account
# is a real one to be managed rather than re-seeded. Changing the password
# later means changing it on the account, not here.
SUPERADMIN_USERNAME=superadmin
SUPERADMIN_PASSWORD=change-me-before-deploying
SUPERADMIN_NAME=Super Admin
